<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-datan"><span class="enc-name">Yujiulü Datan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yujiulü Datan was khagan of the Rouran from 414 to 429.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-futu"><span class="enc-name">Yujiulü Futu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yujiulü Futu was khagan of the Rouran from 506 to 508.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-hulu"><span class="enc-name">Yujiulü Hulü</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yujiulü Hulü was khagan of the Rouran from 410 to 414, succeeding his brother Shelun, the founder of the khaganate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-shelun"><span class="enc-name">Yujiulü Shelun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yujiulü Shelun was the founder of the Rouran Khaganate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuki-hideyasu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuki-hideyasu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuki Hideyasu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuki Hideyasu was the second son of Tokugawa Ieyasu, adopted first by Toyotomi Hideyoshi and then by the Yuki family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuknoom-cheen-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuknoom-cheen-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuknoom Ch'een II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuknoom Ch'een II, also called Yuknoom the Great, was the most powerful king of the Kaan, or snake-head, dynasty at Calakmul, ruling from 636 to about 686.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuknoom-yichaak-kahk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuknoom-yichaak-kahk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuknoom Yich'aak K'ahk'</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuknoom Yich'aak K'ahk' was a king of the Kaan dynasty of the Maya, ruling from Calakmul from 686.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuksom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuksom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuksom</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuksom is a village in western Sikkim, India, where in 1642 three Tibetan lamas consecrated Phuntsog Namgyal as the first Chogyal of Sikkim.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yumjaagiin-tsedenbal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yumjaagiin-tsedenbal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal was the leader of the Mongolian People's Republic for over three decades, as head of the ruling party from 1940 to 1954 and from 1958 to 1984, and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yumten"><span class="enc-name">Yumten</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yumten was one of two claimants to the Tibetan throne after the death of the emperor Langdarma around 842, the other being his half-brother Ösung.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yun-bong-gil"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yun-bong-gil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yun Bong-gil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yun Bong-gil was a Korean independence activist and member of Kim Gu's Korean Patriotic Organisation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yun-gwan"><img class="enc-thumb" src