lass="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ali-kenadid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-ali-kenadid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Ali Kenadid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Ali Kenadid was a Somali ruler who founded the Sultanate of Hobyo on the Indian Ocean coast in the 1870s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Balasaguni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Balasaguni was a Turkic poet and statesman from Balasagun, in modern Kyrgyzstan, under the Kara-Khanid Khanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-bey-of-the-nogai-horde"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Bey of the Nogai Horde</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Bey was ruler (bey) of the Nogai Horde from 1549 to 1554, a son of Musa Bey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-dey"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Dey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Dey was dey of Ottoman Tunisia from 1610 to 1637, the most powerful official of the regency in his time.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-abil-saj"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-abil-saj.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf ibn Abi'l-Saj</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf ibn Abi'l-Saj was the Sajid emir of Azerbaijan, ruling as a largely independent Abbasid governor from 901.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-ahmad-al-bitruji"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf ibn Ahmad al-Bitruji</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf ibn Ahmad al-Bitruji was a local leader in south-western al-Andalus who seized Niebla in 1145, during the revolts against the Almoravids begun by Ibn Qasi in the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-al-hasan"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf ibn al-Hasan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf bin Hasan was a sultan of Mombasa, son of the ruler installed by the Portuguese, who was raised in Goa as a Christian under the name Dom Jerónimo Chingulia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf ibn Tashfin was the leader of the Almoravid Empire who died in 1106.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ii"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf II was an Almohad caliph who reigned from 1214 to 1224, succeeding his father Muhammad al-Nasir after the catastrophic defeat at Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-iv-of-granada"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf IV of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf IV, known as Ibn al-Mawl, was Nasrid emir of Granada for a few months in 1432.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-karamanli"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-karamanli.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Karamanli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Karamanli was pasha of Tripoli from 1795 to 1832, the most powerful ruler of the Karamanli dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-lule"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Lule</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Lule was a Ugandan academic and politician who served as president of Uganda for 68 days in April–June 1979, after the overthrow of Idi Amin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-mahamud-ibrahim"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Mahamud Ibrahim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Mahamud Ibrahim was sultan of the Geledi in southern Somalia in the first half of the 19th century and the most powerful ruler of the Gobroon dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-qadir-khan"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Qadir Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Qadir Khan was a ruler of the Kara-Khanid Khanate based at Kashgar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-shah-chak"><span class="enc-name">Yusuf Shah Chak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusuf Shah Chak was sultan of Kashmir, reigning in 1579 and again from 1580 to 1586, and one of the last rulers of the Chak dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusup-abdrakhmanov"><span class="enc-name">Yusup Abdrakhmanov</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yusup Abdrakhmanov was a Kyrgyz communist who headed the government of the Kirghiz Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic from 1927 to 1933.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-hu"><span class="enc-name">Yuwen Hu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuwen Hu was a Xianbei statesman, nephew of Yuwen Tai, the strongman of Western Wei.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-huaji"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuwen-huaji.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuwen Huaji</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuwen Huaji (died 619) was a Sui dynasty general who in 618 led the coup at Jiangdu (modern Yangzhou) in which Emperor Yang of Sui was strangled.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-jue"><span class="enc-name">Yuwen Jue</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuwen Jue was the first ruler of the Northern Zhou, son of the Western Wei regent Yuwen Tai.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuwen Tai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuwen Tai was a general of Xianbei descent who controlled the western half of the Northern Wei after it split in 534–535, ruling Western Wei as paramount general from…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-yidougui"><span class="enc-name">Yuwen Yidougui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuwen Yidougui was the last chief of the Yuwen Xianbei, a confederation in western Liaoning.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuzhno-sakhalinsk"><span class="enc-name">Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk is the capital of Sakhalin Oblast in the Russian Far East.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yves-devreux"><span class="enc-name">Yves d'Évreux</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yves d'Évreux was a French Capuchin friar who served in the colony of Equinoctial France on the island of Maranhão in Brazil from 1612.</span></a></li></ul>
